concepts.content:
While backpropagation is often derived for a single datapoint $\mathbf{x}$, the total cost function typically minimized is the average of the losses over all datapoints in a training set, $\{\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N$. Due to the linearity of the derivative, the gradient of the average loss is equal to the average of the individual gradients:

$$\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N J_i(\theta)}{\partial \theta} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \frac{\partial J_i(\theta)}{\partial \theta}
\end{aligned}$$

To compute a gradient update for algorithms like stochastic gradient descent, backpropagation is applied in batch mode. This involves running the algorithm over each datapoint in the batch (which can be done in parallel) and then averaging the results. The gradient of a sum of terms is simply the sum of the gradients of each term.

