concepts.content:
A linear layer is defined in the forward direction as $\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}= \mathbf{W} \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}+ \mathbf{b}$. To perform backpropagation, we need the gradients with respect to the inputs and parameters. The local Jacobians are $\mathbf{L}^{\mathbf{x}} = \mathbf{W}$ and $\mathbf{L}^{\mathbf{b}} = \mathbf{I}$. Using the chain rule, the backward signals are:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{g}_{\texttt{in}} &= \mathbf{g}_{\texttt{out}}\mathbf{W}\\
    \frac{\partial J}{\partial \mathbf{b}} &= \mathbf{g}_{\texttt{out}}
\end{aligned}$$

(see figure: The forward and backward matrix multiples for a linear layer.)

For the weight matrix $\mathbf{W}$, the gradient $\frac{\partial J}{\partial \mathbf{W}}$ simplifies to an outer product to avoid large sparse matrix multiplications:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \frac{\partial J}{\partial \mathbf{W}} = \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}\mathbf{g}_{\texttt{out}}
\end{aligned}$$

(see figure: Matrix multiply for parameter gradient of a linear layer.)

(see figure: Linear layer forward and backward.)

These operations are easily implemented using standard matrix multiplication primitives.

